  <section class="research-story" aria-labelledby="packaging-design-loop">
    <p class="story-prelude">Illuminate the box.</p>
    <h2 id="packaging-design-loop">From black-box search to white-box optimization.</h2>
    <p class="story-lead">When we can differentiate the physics, one simulation tells us how a design change affects the result. When each simulation is a costly black box, a learned predictor helps us choose which candidates to test.</p>
    <h3 class="story-topic">Signal and Power Integrity in 2.5D and 3D ICs</h3>

    <div class="story-chapters">
      <article class="story-chapter" id="research-openeye">
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <h3>OpenEYE</h3>
          <p class="chapter-question">Can a link model tell us how to change the wiring?</p>
          <ul><li><strong>Gap.</strong> A simulator could score a layout, but choosing the next layout still took a search.</li><li><strong>Approach.</strong> We differentiated the path from wiring geometry to the receiver’s eye diagram so gradients could guide the layout.</li></ul>
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3831252.3848501">Read the ICCAD paper</a>
        </div>
        <figure class="chapter-figure">
          <img src="{{ '/assets/img/research-projects/openeye.webp' | relative_url }}" width="1319" height="417" loading="lazy" alt="A differentiable chain from interconnect geometry to a statistical eye and a gradient back to trace widths.">
          <figcaption>How trace geometry shapes the receiver’s eye.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="story-chapter" id="research-ml">
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <p class="chapter-bridge">Some evaluations still run through an expensive black box.</p>
          <h3>Keysight · ML Prediction</h3>
          <p class="chapter-question">Can we make those evaluations faster?</p>
          <ul><li><strong>Gap.</strong> Repeated electromagnetic solves slow down candidate exploration.</li><li><strong>Approach.</strong> Built ML predictors for electrical response. Predicted S-parameters can help an optimizer score candidates and select the next circuit to simulate.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="chapter-figure">
          <ol class="method-diagram" aria-label="Design inputs flow through a learned predictor to estimated S-parameters">
            <li>Design inputs</li><li>Learned predictor</li><li>S-parameters</li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption>Predict the electrical response of a candidate circuit.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="story-chapter" id="research-disco">
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <p class="chapter-bridge">A faster score still leaves the question of what to change together.</p>
          <h3>DiSCO</h3>
          <p class="chapter-question">Can we optimize signal and power together?</p>
          <ul><li><strong>Gap.</strong> Tuning signal and power separately can miss how changes in one affect the other.</li><li><strong>Approach.</strong> We brought supply-induced jitter into the eye model and tuned equalizers and drivers against the same margin.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="chapter-figure">
          <img src="{{ '/assets/img/research-projects/disco.webp' | relative_url }}" width="1342" height="331" loading="lazy" alt="DiSCO links transmitter and receiver settings, a coupled signal-power model, and the eye-mask objective.">
          <figcaption>Joint signal and power optimization for receiver eye margin.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="story-chapter" id="research-decap">
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <p class="chapter-bridge">Once the eye included power, it could guide choices inside the power network.</p>
          <h3>BlockCAP</h3>
          <p class="chapter-question">Which capacitor actually improves the link?</p>
          <ul><li><strong>Gap.</strong> A better impedance profile can still leave the receiver with little extra margin.</li><li><strong>Approach.</strong> We differentiate through capacitor selection to the eye, then independently re-evaluate the selected discrete design.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="chapter-figure">
          <div class="result-diagram" role="group" aria-label="Worst-lane eye width rises from 0.486 to 0.741 UI with the selected capacitor">
            <p>Worst-lane eye width</p>
            <div><span>No decap</span><i style="--bar:48.6%" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>0.486 UI</b></div>
            <div><span>Selected decap</span><i style="--bar:74.1%" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>0.741 UI</b></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Eight-lane UCIe-A benchmark at 48 Gb/s.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="story-chapter" id="research-patterns">
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <p class="chapter-bridge">After opening the eye through design, we turned the search around.</p>
          <h3>GPU-Bit</h3>
          <p class="chapter-question">What input would close the eye?</p>
          <ul><li><strong>Gap.</strong> A pattern found with a linear channel model can miss stress caused by supply-dependent timing.</li><li><strong>Approach.</strong> We search with the coupled model and replay the resulting bit sequence in transient simulation.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="chapter-figure">
          <div class="result-diagram" role="group" aria-label="In ADS replay, the linear stress pattern measured 54.7 millivolts of eye height and the supply-aware pattern measured 28.4">
            <p>Eye height in ADS replay</p>
            <div><span>Linear pattern</span><i style="--bar:91.2%" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>54.7 mV</b></div>
            <div><span>Supply-aware</span><i style="--bar:47.3%" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>28.4 mV</b></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>One 48 GT/s test condition with background activity.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="story-chapter" id="research-drift">
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <p class="chapter-bridge">In 3D ICs, a power-network edit can change what needs to be checked.</p>
          <h3>DRIFT</h3>
          <p class="chapter-question">Which moments do we need to simulate again?</p>
          <ul><li><strong>Gap.</strong> Activity-based screening can miss the windows with the largest voltage drops.</li><li><strong>Approach.</strong> We are testing power-network-aware screening to decide which windows warrant an exact transient solve after a design change.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="chapter-figure">
          <img src="{{ '/assets/img/research-projects/drift.webp' | relative_url }}" width="910" height="468" loading="lazy" alt="A benchmark plot showing how many windows power-based screening needs to capture the ten worst voltage-drop windows.">
          <figcaption>How many windows must a power-based ranking inspect to find the ten largest voltage drops?</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="story-chapter" id="research-mosaic">
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <p class="chapter-bridge">We brought the same question about where to spend compute to routing.</p>
          <h3>MosaicGR</h3>
          <p class="chapter-question">How should CPUs and GPUs share the work?</p>
          <ul><li><strong>Gap.</strong> Routes proposed in parallel can compete for the same routing space.</li><li><strong>Approach.</strong> GPUs generate candidates, then CPUs score and accept them against the latest congestion state.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="chapter-figure">
          <ol class="method-diagram" aria-label="Parallel GPU proposals go through a CPU check against live congestion before routes are accepted">
            <li>GPU proposes</li><li>CPU re-scores</li><li>Routes update</li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption>Parallel proposals, sequential acceptance.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="story-chapter" id="research-agents">
        <div class="chapter-copy">
          <p class="chapter-bridge">The next question brings us back to Keysight and the whole workflow.</p>
          <h3>Keysight · Agentic Workflow</h3>
          <p class="chapter-question">Can an agent turn a design question into a workflow engineers can test?</p>
          <ul><li><strong>Gap.</strong> A predictor can score a candidate. For each new problem, engineers still have to prepare the data, choose a model, and set up the optimization.</li><li><strong>Next step.</strong> Build an LLM agent that proposes a setup engineers can inspect, revise, and rerun. Compare its results with an expert setup under the same compute budget.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="chapter-figure">
          <ol class="method-diagram" aria-label="The agent proposes a setup, an engineer reviews it, and the workflow is benchmarked">
            <li>Propose a setup</li><li>Review and run</li><li>Benchmark</li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption>Compare task completion and model quality against an expert baseline.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>
    </div>
